<section class="wrap narrow reveal editorial-manifesto" aria-labelledby="reader-contract-title">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The clarity edition</p>
    <h2 id="reader-contract-title">A Reader&rsquo;s Contract</h2>
    <p class="lede">This project asks for imaginative attention, but never for unearned agreement. Every
      research page now opens by saying what it argues, what established work can support, exactly where the
      inference stops, and which question puts the greatest pressure on it.</p>
    <p><strong>Mathematics is not used as a costume for certainty.</strong> An exact identity may constrain a
      model without proving the model&rsquo;s physical interpretation. A Möbius band can reverse orientation
      without reversing time; a compactified boundary can organise a diagram without becoming an event; a
      numerical pattern can be exact without being a law of nature.</p>
    <p><strong>External sources are bearings, not endorsements.</strong> The source notes point to the strongest
      established account or relevant live dispute &mdash; from general relativity and cosmological parameter
      inference to consciousness research, entropy, little red dots and the Riemann Hypothesis. None of those
      sources is presented as supporting TTI unless it actually does.</p>
    <p><strong>A claim earns force by becoming easier to lose.</strong> Where the framework lacks units, a carrier,
      an action, a conservation law or a discriminating observation, the prose says so. The aim of this edition
      is not to make speculation sound safer. It is to make each proposal clear enough to challenge.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap archive-preserved-plate reveal" aria-labelledby="t-the-chain-of-necessity">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Preserved introductory plate</p>
    <h2 id="t-the-chain-of-necessity">The Proposed Chain of Dependence</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Six links to test in order. The mathematical relations can be exact while the
      ontological promotions between them remain choices; the chain is only as strong as its weakest bridge.</p>
  </div>

  <figure id="fig-chain" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate I</span> Zero and One, Closed Through π
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></figcaption>
    <div class="plate-art">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 900 300" role="img" aria-label="A chain of six linked stages running from zero and one, through pi, a quarter-turn to i, the complex plane, the wavefunction, and matter">
        <defs>
          <marker id="ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="#76cfee"/>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g font-family="Cinzel, serif" font-size="13" fill="#cdefff" text-anchor="middle">
          <circle cx="90" cy="120" r="46" fill="none" stroke="#76cfee" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="90" y="118">0 &amp; 1</text>
          <text x="90" y="138" font-size="9" fill="#a9b6d8">two infinities</text>

          <circle cx="250" cy="120" r="40" fill="none" stroke="#45d6b4" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="250" y="125" font-size="20">&#960;</text>

          <circle cx="400" cy="120" r="40" fill="none" stroke="#45d6b4" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="400" y="118" font-size="18">i</text>
          <text x="400" y="136" font-size="8" fill="#a9b6d8">&#960;/2</text>

          <circle cx="550" cy="120" r="44" fill="none" stroke="#a07cf0" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="550" y="118" font-size="11">complex</text>
          <text x="550" y="133" font-size="11">plane</text>

          <circle cx="700" cy="120" r="44" fill="none" stroke="#a07cf0" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="700" y="124" font-size="18">&#968;</text>

          <circle cx="840" cy="120" r="40" fill="none" stroke="#ef84b6" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="840" y="125" font-size="12">matter</text>
        </g>
        <g stroke="#76cfee" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#ar)" opacity="0.8">
          <path d="M138 120 H206"/><path d="M292 120 H356"/><path d="M442 120 H502"/>
          <path d="M596 120 H652"/><path d="M746 120 H796"/>
        </g>
        <g font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="11" fill="#a9b6d8" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">
          <text x="172" y="106">must curve</text>
          <text x="324" y="106">halve it</text>
          <text x="472" y="106">unfolds</text>
          <text x="624" y="106">houses</text>
          <text x="771" y="106">is</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">In the standard complex plane, \(e^{i\pi/2}=i\) marks a quarter-turn from the
      real axis to the imaginary axis. Complex phase is indispensable to the usual quantum formalism and to
      its account of interference. Network experiments can distinguish that theory from particular
      real-amplitude rivals, although newer work shows that equivalent real-arithmetic representations remain
      possible when the composition rules are changed. TTI&rsquo;s further claim &mdash; that the quarter-turn
      explains why nature is quantum &mdash; remains an interpretation, not an experimental result.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </figure>
</section>

